les and CI config are not in any file scope: tests are written separately.\n- stubs: for every NEW public type/method/endpoint the tests will call, give a compilable stub file (full file content) whose bodies throw new Error(\"not implemented\"), so tests compile before implementation. The stubs are built as they are, on the untouched code, before anything is implemented: a stub may only use types the repo already has or that another stub declares (an interface the endpoint classes share is a stub of its own). Existing APIs need no stubs. Stub paths must be inside a task's fileScope.\n- Outside systems (an email or SMS provider, a payment service, another company's API, a file store): the code reaches each one through one seam, a module of its own (one exported function per call it makes), and nothing else in the app calls the system directly. Use the seam the repo already has for that system; for a new one, give the seam as a stub, so the tests can put a stand-in behind it before the code exists. The system's settings (host, port, sender address, key) are read from configuration by name: put .env.example in the fileScope of the task that writes the seam's real implementation, and say in its approach that each new setting is declared there with an empty value.\n- protectedPathsDeclared: list any migration, CI, build-config or package-feed file you must change (a human will see it).\n- newDependencies: any npm package to add (name, version, registry). Prefer none.\n\nAPI CONTRACT. This product has a web app and an API in two repos, and both are built against one contract.\n- Give one more stub: path \"contracts/openapi.yaml\", content the full OpenAPI 3.0.3 document (YAML) of every operation the approved screens and requirements need. It is outside every task's fileScope: nobody implements it, both sides follow it.\n- Write it in block style, one key per line with two-space indents: no {...} or [...] on a line except an empty value (\"security: []\", \"{}\"). Put any